            <ArticleTitle>Examining the Weight Trajectory of College Students.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>137-141.e1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1499-4046(16)30872-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jneb.2016.10.014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the weight trajectory of students over 4 years of college.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Anthropometric assessments were completed at the beginning and end of students' freshman year and the end of senior year to calculate body mass index. Questionnaires assessing weight-related behaviors were completed in senior year.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the original 117 students, 86 remained in the study for 4 years. Body mass index was significantly higher at the end of senior year (mean, 24.84; SD, 4.46) vs the beginning of freshman year (mean, 23.59; SD, 4.01; t[85] = 5.61; P &lt; .001). Weight was significantly higher at the end of senior year (mean, 71.32 kg; SD, 15.60) vs the beginning of freshman year (mean, 66.94 kg; SD, 14.02; t[85] = 6.60; P &lt; .001). Students' mean weight gain was 4.38 kg and the sample increased from 23% to 41% overweight/obese. No significant associations were found between BMI and lifestyle factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS AND IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study suggests that students gain weight throughout college, which highlights the need for weight control interventions to target more than just freshman college students.</AbstractText>
